From d58b156b56fb146a97a95872b880243a3d4a55b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: haywoodspartan <6591811+haywoodspartan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 02:59:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rpc: mesh communicator, socket allreduce for any number of
 servers

Tensor parallelism over RPC reduced across servers only with a backend
communicator (NCCL) or, without one, between exactly two servers over a
star rooted at rank 0. The star also made every server reach rank 0 on
the address the client uses for it, which is the wrong interface when
each pair of servers has its own link.

Add a mesh communicator: every rank holds a socket to every other rank,
and the socket reduction covers any world size.

- COMM_ADDRS: a server reports the IPv4 addresses of its interfaces,
  with prefix length and link speed.
- COMM_INIT_MESH: the client tells the higher rank of each pair where to
  reach the lower one, picking an address on a subnet both report
  (fastest link first, then the most specific subnet). Each link runs
  the usual caps handshake, so it upgrades to RDMA on its own interface.
- The reduction sends decode-sized partials to all peers before it
  receives any, and exchanges larger ones with all peers at once, lower
  rank first. Every rank adds the partials in rank order.
- Servers advertise the feature in the former padding byte of the HELLO
  response. The client uses the mesh only when every server has it, so
  stock peers keep the star. GGML_RPC_NO_COMM_MESH forces the star.
- Each rank logs where its reductions spent their time when the
  communicator is freed.

With two ranks the output is identical to the pairwise path. With three
ranks perplexity is within 0.04% of the client-side fallback. On three
DGX Spark nodes cabled as a ring, all three links come up on RDMA and a
111B model generates 6.47 t/s on three ranks against 2.63 on one node.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
